{{/if}}

## A deterministic core

The surface rests on the fixed-step core `specs/movement.md` fixes: the
simulation advances in whole `TICK_DT` ticks at `TICK_HZ` (`120`) of them a
second, so a tick is the unit the operations below count in. Two more properties
make the game driveable from code:

- Render-free core. Game state advances from the elapsed time the game is handed,
  independent of a canvas, of the frame loop that measured it, and of wall-clock
  time. The dependency runs one way: the simulation reads nothing from the
  renderer.
- Seeded randomness. Every draw the game makes, for predator and drifter wander,
  for the bonus-drifter cadence, and for the maze it lays out, runs off a
  generator the surface's `reset` seeds, and the game keeps that generator's whole
  state, so reseeding and replaying the same calls reproduces the same result
  exactly.

Given the same seed and the same sequence of calls and elapsed game time, the game
reaches the same state every time.

## What the runtime provides instead

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The keyboard and the overlay belong to the runtime layer beneath the game, and the
surface carries no operation for either. That layer delivers the key bindings this
specification fixes, so a dispatched keyboard event drives the forager, the sonar
pulse, the ink, the menus, the pause and the mute exactly as a player's key does,
and it draws and toggles the debug overlay.

The clock is the exception, because nothing outside this build owns it. The
surface is what takes the game off real time and advances it, through
`setAutoStep` and `advance` below.
{{else}}
The clock, the keyboard and the overlay belong to the {{engine.name}} engine,
which documents them in the `engine/` directory at the root of this repository,
and the surface carries no operation for any of them. The engine advances the game
frame by frame and a scripted clock takes it off real time, it drives the actions
the game registers so a dispatched keyboard event moves the forager exactly as a
player's key does, and it draws and toggles the debug overlay.
{{/if}}

What the overlay shows is Fathom's, under Diagnostics below.

## The operations

The surface carries `version` (`FATHOM_DEBUG_VERSION`, `1`), a plain number, and
the operations below. Values are plain numbers, strings and booleans. Positions
and radii are in the logical units `specs/overview.md` fixes, `tx` and `ty` are
the column and row of the tile grid defined there, and every duration is in
seconds.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}

Each operation's first parameter is the current state, `DeepReadonly<FathomState>`.
A pose returns the next `FathomState`, built from the one it was handed, which it
leaves as it was; a reading returns what it read. The headings below name the
parameters that follow the state.
{{/if}}

An argument outside the domain its operation states is invalid, and the call fails
loudly rather than guessing what was meant.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `setAutoStep(enabled)`

Takes the game off real time, and gives it back. `setAutoStep(false)` stops the
frame loop advancing the simulation from the wall clock, so the game changes only
when `advance` says so. `setAutoStep(true)` returns it to running itself, which is
how a build starts and how it is played.

Drawing is unaffected either way: the loop keeps rendering, so the canvas shows
the state the most recent tick left.

### `advance(ticks)`

Runs `ticks` whole simulation ticks immediately and in order, each worth exactly
one `TICK_DT`. The unit is ticks rather than seconds, so nothing is rounded: `advance(1)`
runs one tick and `advance(120)` covers one second of game time. Each tick is a real
tick, the same one the frame loop runs, so the game's own sensing, pathfinding and
contact rules produce the result, and the canvas is redrawn to reflect it.

`ticks` is a non-negative whole number, and `advance(0)` runs nothing.

Advancing while the game is still stepping automatically adds to what the wall
clock is already doing, so a reproducible scenario calls `setAutoStep(false)`
first.

{{/if}}
{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `reset(state, options)`
{{else}}
### `reset(options)`
{{/if}}

Restores every field of the observable state defined in `specs/state.md` to its
title-screen value: the title screen with its first menu item highlighted, the
score at `0`, `START_LIVES` (`3`) lives, depth `1`, brightness `0`, a freshly laid
out maze with plankton on every corridor tile, no drifters, every predator in the
den and unreleased, both cooldowns ready, no pulses and no ink clouds in flight,
the fog fully unrevealed, creature minds on, and `simTime` at `0`.
`options.seed` seeds the game's random generator, defaulting to `DEFAULT_SEED`
(`1`).
`options` is optional and an omitted `seed` takes the default.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
A reset also re-arms manual stepping, leaving the game off the wall clock.
{{/if}}

`muted` is untouched, because muting is a player preference rather than a value a
dive opens with.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `snapshot(state)`

A pure reading of `state`, returned as the plain object `specs/state.md` defines.
It returns no state, because it poses nothing.

### `startDive(state)`
{{else}}
### `snapshot()`

A pure read of the game, returned as the plain object `specs/state.md` defines. It
changes nothing.

### `startDive()`
{{/if}}

Poses the opening of a real dive, exactly as choosing `DIVE` from the title menu
does: the score returns to `0`, lives to `START_LIVES`, depth to `1`, the maze is
laid out afresh with its full plankton, every predator is in the den and
unreleased, and `screen` becomes `"countdown"`. It leaves `simTime` as it is.

The dive runs from there: the countdown ticks down against the elapsed time and
play begins through the game's own code.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `beginPlay(state)`
{{else}}
### `beginPlay()`
{{/if}}

Ends the dive countdown immediately instead of waiting it out, so `screen` becomes
`"playing"` and the den's release schedule starts from that moment. It applies on
the `"countdown"` screen alone.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setDepth(state, d)`
{{else}}
### `setDepth(d)`
{{/if}}

Sets the current depth to the whole number `d`, at least `1`. Everything depth
scales recomputes from it, so the predator roster and the sonar range are those
`specs/progression.md` and `specs/predators.md` give for depth `d`, and a caller
reads the scaled values back from the snapshot. It changes neither the maze nor
the screen.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setMaze(state, rows)`
{{else}}
### `setMaze(rows)`
{{/if}}

Replaces the maze layout with the one given. `rows` is an array of `GRID_ROWS`
(`18`) strings of `GRID_COLS` (`36`) characters each, in exactly the alphabet the
snapshot's `tiles` uses: `'#'` rock, `'.'` corridor, `'g'` the den gate, and `'d'`
den interior. A layout of the wrong size, carrying any other character, or
carrying `'d'` or `'g'` tiles without exactly one `'g'`, is invalid.

The posed layout is a fixture and is used exactly as given. It is **exempt** from
every rule in `specs/maze.md`: dead ends, corridors wider than one tile,
asymmetry, disconnected regions, a missing wrap tunnel and a missing den are all
legal in a fixture, and the game keeps running on one. A structure the layout does
not have simply takes no part: with no den gate the bonus-drifter cadence has
nowhere to admit a drifter from and admits none, and a predator returned to a den
that is not there is held out of play. The game accepts the fixture as it stands,
without repairing it, refusing it, stalling on it, or returning to a maze of its
own.

After the call the game behaves in every respect as though the posed layout were
the maze it had laid out itself. The wrap tunnel of a posed layout is the first
row, if any, whose column `0` and column `35` tiles are both open.

The board is left in the state a freshly laid-out maze starts in: a plankton on
every corridor tile outside the den, revealed-tile memory cleared back to fog, and
every predator returned to a den tile with its `released` flag `false`. The
staggered release schedule is **suspended** while a posed board stands, so no
release time arrives and no predator leaves the den until a later
`setPredatorState` poses it out. The forager is placed at rest on the first
corridor tile in reading order, scanning the top row left to right and then each
row below, which is a defined resting place rather than a meaningful one.

What it does not change is what the game is doing. The score, the lives, the
depth, the cooldowns and the screen are all left as they are, so a call made
during live play leaves the game in live play. The fixture holds until the next
`reset`, or until a descent lays out the next maze.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setForagerTile(state, tx, ty)`
{{else}}
### `setForagerTile(tx, ty)`
{{/if}}

Moves the forager to the center of tile `(tx, ty)` and leaves it at rest there, as
though no movement key were held. The tile is an open corridor tile. Its facing is
untouched, and injected input moves it from there through the game's ordinary
movement code.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setForagerDir(state, dir)`
{{else}}
### `setForagerDir(dir)`
{{/if}}

Sets the forager's facing to `dir`, one of `"up"`, `"down"`, `"left"` and
`"right"`. It moves the forager nowhere and leaves it at rest.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setBrightness(state, g)`
{{else}}
### `setBrightness(g)`
{{/if}}

Sets the forager's brightness `G` to `g`, in `[0, 1]`. Everything derived from
brightness recomputes from it.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "kindle")}}
The light radius `V`, the vision-circle radius `R`, and the Lanternjaw's and
Flarefish's light detection ranges are those `specs/sensing.md`,
`specs/predators/lanternjaw.md` and `specs/predators/flarefish.md` give for `g`.
{{else}}
The light radius `V` and the Lanternjaw's and Flarefish's light detection ranges
are those `specs/sensing.md`, `specs/predators/lanternjaw.md` and
`specs/predators/flarefish.md` give for `g`.
{{/if}}

It also arms the `BRIGHT_HOLD` (`1.0 s`) brightness hold, exactly as eating a
plankton does, so the value it poses is steady for that full second and only then
decays on the ordinary curve. The hold is armed in full every time, so this
operation poses neither a part-spent hold nor a forager with no hold at all.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setPredatorTile(state, index, tx, ty)`
{{else}}
### `setPredatorTile(index, tx, ty)`
{{/if}}

Moves one predator to the center of tile `(tx, ty)` and leaves it there. `index`
selects a predator by its position in the snapshot's `predators` list, counted
from `0`. The tile is an open corridor tile, a den tile or the den gate. Its
facing, its state and its `released` flag are untouched.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setPredatorDir(state, index, dir)`
{{else}}
### `setPredatorDir(index, dir)`
{{/if}}

Sets one predator's facing to `dir`, one of `"up"`, `"down"`, `"left"` and
`"right"`. `index` selects the predator as above.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setPredatorState(state, index, value)`
{{else}}
### `setPredatorState(index, value)`
{{/if}}

Sets one predator's state to `value`, one of `"den"`, `"wander"` and `"chase"`.
`index` selects the predator as above. `"search"` is reached only through the
Gloamfin's own behavior and is not posed. After the pose the predator's own mind
runs whenever the simulation advances: it senses, acquires, chases and searches
through the real code.

| `value` | What it poses |
| --- | --- |
| `"den"` | The predator is returned to a den tile, its `released` flag becomes `false`, and its release time is **suspended**, so it stays in the den chamber however long the scenario runs, until a later call poses it out. On a board with no den chamber it is held out of play, undrawn, unmoving, and unable to make contact. |
| `"wander"` | The predator is loose on the tile it stands on, patrolling, with no fix on the forager. Its `released` flag becomes `true`. |
| `"chase"` | The predator is loose on the tile it stands on, with a fix on the forager's current tile, pursuing it. Its `released` flag becomes `true`. |

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `spawnDrifter(state, tx, ty)`
{{else}}
### `spawnDrifter(tx, ty)`
{{/if}}

Adds one bonus drifter at the center of tile `(tx, ty)`, an open corridor tile. It
then wanders through the ordinary drifter code and is worth the ordinary bonus
when eaten. It is added whatever the drifter cadence is doing and whatever the
`DRIFTER_MAX` (`2`) ceiling would otherwise allow.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setCreatureAI(state, enabled)`
{{else}}
### `setCreatureAI(enabled)`
{{/if}}

Turns the creatures' own minds on or off, where a creature is every predator and
every bonus drifter. They are on by default and a `reset` restores them.

With them off, each creature holds exactly where it stands, keeping the tile,
facing and state it was posed with: it senses nothing, decides nothing, and does
not move, however long the scenario runs. Everything else continues. The forager
still swims under its own controls, its brightness and light still follow what it
eats, cooldowns still run down, sonar wavefronts and ink clouds still travel and
expire, plankton and drifters are still eaten and still score, and contact with a
predator still costs a life.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setPlankton(state, tx, ty, present)`
{{else}}
### `setPlankton(tx, ty, present)`
{{/if}}

Puts a plankton on tile `(tx, ty)` when `present` is `true` and takes one off when
it is `false`, and adjusts `planktonRemaining` to match. The tile is an open
corridor tile. Removing a plankton this way is not eating it, so it scores nothing
and clears no maze.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `clearPlankton(state)`
{{else}}
### `clearPlankton()`
{{/if}}

Takes every plankton off the maze at once, leaving `planktonRemaining` at `0`. As
with `setPlankton`, nothing here is eaten, so it scores nothing and clears no
maze: the maze is cleared by the forager eating a plankton when none remain after
it, and an empty maze the forager has not just eaten from stays in live play.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setSonarCooldown(state, seconds)`
{{else}}
### `setSonarCooldown(seconds)`
{{/if}}

Sets the seconds remaining on the sonar pulse's cooldown, at least `0`. At `0` the
pulse is ready and the snapshot reports `sonar.ready` as `true`. The cooldown runs
down from the posed value on the ordinary curve.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setInkCooldown(state, seconds)`
{{else}}
### `setInkCooldown(seconds)`
{{/if}}

Sets the seconds remaining on ink's cooldown, at least `0`. At `0` ink is ready and
the snapshot reports `ink.ready` as `true`. The cooldown runs down from the posed
value on the ordinary curve.

## Diagnostics

The debug overlay is read-only. It shows the values the game registers with it as
diagnostic sources, it draws over the running game without changing anything, and
it is hidden until it is toggled. Register at least the current `screen` and
`depth`, the `score` and `lives`, the brightness `G`, the light radius `V`,
{{#if (eq variant.slug "kindle")}}the vision-circle radius `R`, {{/if}}the sonar and ink cooldowns, the plankton
remaining, the forager's tile and facing, and, for each predator, its kind,
state, tile, speed and whether its alert is firing. Those are the same facts the
snapshot reports. Keep each source short enough to read on a line and keep every
one a pure read, so watching the overlay leaves the simulation as it is.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The overlay is part of the runtime layer you write. It draws the registered
sources in the game's monospace type, plainly and clearly apart from the HUD, and
the backtick key (`KeyboardEvent.code` `Backquote`) shows and hides it. It starts
hidden.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Registering those values is the whole of Fathom's part, through
`InitApi.diagnostics`. Each source is called with the state current at the read, as
`DeepReadonly<FathomState>`, and reports the value that state carries. Drawing the
panel, toggling it with the backtick key, and keeping it read-only are the
engine's.
{{else}}
Registering those values is the whole of Fathom's part, through the diagnostics
registries the engine provides. Each source is a function of no arguments, called
at each read, reporting the live game at the moment it runs. Drawing the panel,
toggling it with the backtick key, and keeping it read-only are the engine's.
{{/if}}
